1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,160
 What does AI memory actually mean?

2
00:00:03,160 --> 00:00:05,480
 That's the question for this episode.

3
00:00:05,480 --> 00:00:12,160
 Hello, I'm Russell and welcome to episode 11 of The Articulate Journal where I'm exploring

4
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:16,720
 what it takes to build an AI-native architecture from first principles.

5
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:21,440
 The previous episode, the question was about AI runtimes and what do we need to understand

6
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:26,840
 in order to choose one, which led to the question for this episode.

7
00:00:26,840 --> 00:00:33,160
 Every AI framework seems to talk about memory, working memory, conversation memory, long-term

8
00:00:33,160 --> 00:00:36,280
 memory, execution memory and knowledge memory.

9
00:00:36,280 --> 00:00:41,520
 At first glance, it sounds as though memory has become one of the most important concepts

10
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:43,520
 in AI-native systems.

11
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:50,400
 But when you start researching, you instinctively pause and ask yourself, "Hold on, isn't this

12
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:51,400
 just?

13
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:52,400
 You've got it.

14
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:54,760
 Something we already know by another name."

15
00:00:54,760 --> 00:00:59,320
 That's not me being cynical, that's the industry that we're in.

16
00:00:59,320 --> 00:01:04,440
 We naturally try to decompose ideas until we understand the responsibilities underneath

17
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:05,920
 them.

18
00:01:05,920 --> 00:01:10,000
 And that's what I wanted to do with this episode, because I think the word "memory" is hiding

19
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,960
 something much more interesting.

20
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:19,680
 If you're building an AI-native platform, understanding memory matters, questions immediately start

21
00:01:19,680 --> 00:01:21,160
 appearing.

22
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:26,760
 Where should conversations be stored, in my case, for articulate, where does architectural

23
00:01:26,760 --> 00:01:28,820
 knowledge live?

24
00:01:28,820 --> 00:01:34,080
 What embeddings should be persisted, or should they be persisted at all?

25
00:01:34,080 --> 00:01:36,520
 What happens if the runtime crashes?

26
00:01:36,520 --> 00:01:40,080
 Can execution continue tomorrow?

27
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:41,840
 Those aren't academic questions.

28
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:43,680
 They shape the architecture.

29
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:45,440
 So let's ask a different question.

30
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:52,160
 Instead of asking what memory does AI systems need, let's ask what state does AI-native

31
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:55,880
 platforms actually need?

32
00:01:55,880 --> 00:01:59,960
 This is where things become confusing, because different frameworks use the word "memory"

33
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:03,560
 to describe completely different things.

34
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:10,320
 Sometimes memory means conversation history, vector databases, retrieve documents, execution

35
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:16,600
 checkpoints, prompts, context, knowledge graphs, and caches.

36
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:20,160
 They're all classed as memory, but are they actually the same thing?

37
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:22,000
 I don't think so.

38
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:25,320
 And that's where architecture starts to become clearer.

39
00:02:25,320 --> 00:02:28,400
 Let's separate those responsibilities.

40
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:31,440
 Conversation history remembers what we've talked about.

41
00:02:31,440 --> 00:02:35,400
 Architectural knowledge represents what the system knows.

42
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:40,640
 Languages and vector indexes exist to retrieve information efficiently.

43
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:46,800
 Caches improve performance, and execution state remembers work already performed.

44
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:50,880
 Configuration describes how the system behaves.

45
00:02:50,880 --> 00:02:55,320
 They're all storing information, but they have completely different purposes, different

46
00:02:55,320 --> 00:02:58,400
 owners, lifetimes, and rules.

47
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:03,120
 Calling them all memory hides those differences.

48
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:07,320
 There's one other thing that is frequently described as memory.

49
00:03:07,320 --> 00:03:08,320
 Context.

50
00:03:08,320 --> 00:03:11,560
 And this one, I believe, is very different.

51
00:03:11,560 --> 00:03:15,600
 When an agent is reasoning, it needs a working set of information.

52
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:20,120
 That might include parts of the conversation, information retrieved from an information

53
00:03:20,120 --> 00:03:25,920
 system, knowledge from the knowledge model in the context of Articulate.

54
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:31,600
 The context could include results from previous tool calls, instructions for the current task,

55
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:35,320
 and maybe even information produced by another agent.

56
00:03:35,320 --> 00:03:40,760
 Together, these things form the context available to the model at that moment.

57
00:03:40,760 --> 00:03:43,680
 But I don't think context is another source of truth.

58
00:03:43,680 --> 00:03:44,680
 It's assembled.

59
00:03:44,680 --> 00:03:49,240
 It's a temporary view of information needed for a particular piece of reasoning.

60
00:03:49,240 --> 00:03:52,760
 And importantly, much of that could be reconstructed.

61
00:03:52,760 --> 00:03:56,440
 If I lose the context, I can retrieve that knowledge again.

62
00:03:56,440 --> 00:03:58,080
 I can reload the conversations.

63
00:03:58,080 --> 00:03:59,920
 I can rebuild the instructions.

64
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:03,880
 I can call it a tool again, providing that that's safe.

65
00:04:03,880 --> 00:04:07,320
 So context matters enormously to the quality of reasoning.

66
00:04:07,320 --> 00:04:11,840
 But architecturally, I don't think we need another database called memory, just to own

67
00:04:11,840 --> 00:04:13,320
 it.

68
00:04:13,320 --> 00:04:18,200
 What we need is a way of engineering the right context for the state that already has an

69
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:20,720
 appropriate home.

70
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:24,040
 That gives me another useful distinction.

71
00:04:24,040 --> 00:04:26,000
 Knowledge is what the platform knows.

72
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:31,680
 Conversation is what we've discussed, and context is what the model needs right now.

73
00:04:31,680 --> 00:04:36,480
 Once I stop thinking about memory as one thing, another pattern appeared.

74
00:04:36,480 --> 00:04:39,600
 Every piece of state naturally belongs somewhere.

75
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:44,240
 The knowledge model owns architectural knowledge in the case of Articulate.

76
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:48,440
 A conversation service owns conversations.

77
00:04:48,440 --> 00:04:54,120
 The retrieval layer owns embeddings, vector indexes, and graph projections.

78
00:04:54,120 --> 00:04:59,160
 Ashes belong wherever performance needs improving.

79
00:04:59,160 --> 00:05:02,980
 Capability definitions and prompts belong to configuration.

80
00:05:02,980 --> 00:05:06,960
 And the runtime owns surprisingly little.

81
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:12,160
 Instead, it coordinates all those platform capabilities that completely changed how I

82
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:16,080
 started to think about the runtime architecture.

83
00:05:16,080 --> 00:05:19,720
 One distinction became surprisingly important.

84
00:05:19,720 --> 00:05:26,360
 Often AI systems often contain vector stalls, embeddings, search indexes, graph projections.

85
00:05:26,360 --> 00:05:31,840
 They're all incredibly useful, but architecturally, they're not the knowledge.

86
00:05:31,840 --> 00:05:36,240
 If Redis disappears, we don't panic, we rebuild the cache.

87
00:05:36,240 --> 00:05:40,920
 The same is true for many AI native optimizations.

88
00:05:40,920 --> 00:05:45,080
 Prompt caches, embedding caches, retrieval context caches.

89
00:05:45,080 --> 00:05:47,400
 They're all valuable because they improve performance.

90
00:05:47,400 --> 00:05:50,960
 They're not valuable because they contain the truth.

91
00:05:50,960 --> 00:05:55,920
 And if stale caches aren't managed carefully, they can become very dangerous.

92
00:05:55,920 --> 00:06:01,720
 An AI system may confidently reason over outdated information simply because the cache hasn't

93
00:06:01,720 --> 00:06:03,720
 been refreshed.

94
00:06:03,720 --> 00:06:10,080
 So caching remains just what it always has been, a performance optimization, not a source

95
00:06:10,080 --> 00:06:12,640
 of truth.

96
00:06:12,640 --> 00:06:15,880
 Something else became obvious during this investigation.

97
00:06:15,880 --> 00:06:18,280
 We've understood this principle for decades.

98
00:06:18,280 --> 00:06:21,280
 We don't store every kind of data in one database.

99
00:06:21,280 --> 00:06:26,640
 We choose storage based on the responsibility of that data.

100
00:06:26,640 --> 00:06:30,320
 Knowledge may suit one model, conversation has another.

101
00:06:30,320 --> 00:06:34,560
 Vectors, execution, caches all have different responsibilities.

102
00:06:34,560 --> 00:06:39,720
 AI native systems aren't using multiple storage technologies because AI is special.

103
00:06:39,720 --> 00:06:46,320
 They're doing it because different kinds of state have fundamentally different responsibilities.

104
00:06:46,320 --> 00:06:48,080
 Architecture hasn't changed.

105
00:06:48,080 --> 00:06:51,440
 The kinds of state have.

106
00:06:51,440 --> 00:06:55,200
 By this point, I realized something I wasn't expecting.

107
00:06:55,200 --> 00:06:58,720
 The runtime isn't the brain of the platform, it's a coordinator.

108
00:06:58,720 --> 00:07:00,440
 It doesn't own the architecture.

109
00:07:00,440 --> 00:07:04,880
 It doesn't own the conversations and it doesn't own the retrieval.

110
00:07:04,880 --> 00:07:08,800
 And it doesn't need to permanently own the context either.

111
00:07:08,800 --> 00:07:14,920
 Next can be assembled from the platform when reasoning needs to happen.

112
00:07:14,920 --> 00:07:18,600
 The runtime coordinates all of those capabilities.

113
00:07:18,600 --> 00:07:22,480
 But that leaves one kind of state that is different.

114
00:07:22,480 --> 00:07:23,480
 Execution.

115
00:07:23,480 --> 00:07:29,080
 Because this is the difference between reconstructing what an agent needs to know and reconstructing

116
00:07:29,080 --> 00:07:31,240
 what has already happened.

117
00:07:31,240 --> 00:07:35,720
 If an agent retrieves some information twice, that's probably fine.

118
00:07:35,720 --> 00:07:41,040
 If it sends the same notification twice, updates the same external system twice or performs

119
00:07:41,040 --> 00:07:44,680
 the same action twice, that's a very different problem.

120
00:07:44,680 --> 00:07:48,560
 And that is where memory stops being an interesting question.

121
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:55,080
 The interesting question becomes, how do we preserve the progress of the work?

122
00:07:55,080 --> 00:07:59,640
 We started this episode asking, what does AI memory actually mean?

123
00:07:59,640 --> 00:08:03,720
 And I think the answer is it depends on what you're remembering.

124
00:08:03,720 --> 00:08:07,840
 Which has its own responsibility, so does conversation.

125
00:08:07,840 --> 00:08:11,120
 Retrieval structures and caches can be rebuilt.

126
00:08:11,120 --> 00:08:14,520
 Context is assembled for the reasoning taking place right now.

127
00:08:14,520 --> 00:08:20,280
 Once those responsibilities have been separated, the runtime actually owns surprisingly little.

128
00:08:20,280 --> 00:08:24,920
 But there is one thing we cannot simply reconstruct, the progress of the work.

129
00:08:24,920 --> 00:08:29,960
 Imagine an agent has already called it all, updated some state and asked a human for approval

130
00:08:29,960 --> 00:08:32,520
 and then it stopped.

131
00:08:32,520 --> 00:08:37,400
 When it starts again tomorrow, knowing the conversation isn't enough, knowing the architecture

132
00:08:37,400 --> 00:08:41,520
 isn't enough, even rebuilding exactly the same context isn't enough.

133
00:08:41,520 --> 00:08:46,160
 It needs to know what's already happened and what should happen next, that's a different

134
00:08:46,160 --> 00:08:47,160
 problem.

135
00:08:47,160 --> 00:08:52,600
 It's not really about memory anymore, it's about preserving execution across time.

136
00:08:52,600 --> 00:08:55,080
 And that's the question of the next episode.

137
00:08:55,080 --> 00:09:00,360
 How does an AI native runtime preserve execution across time?

138
00:09:00,360 --> 00:09:05,180
 That's where we get into durable execution, which is the topic for episode 12.

139
00:09:05,180 --> 00:09:05,880
 See you on the next one.

